{# THE ORGANIZATION RIGHT NOW — FOUR FIGURES, like every figure row in the
   product (ruled: four to a row).

   THE ROW IS THE MODULES', AND THE ORGANIZATION ONLY FILLS IT. The design's
   four are a module's each — on duty, patrols out, open incidents, files
   kept — and none is the host's. The organization's own "Areas" tile is
   there because a row of one on a fresh installation says nothing: it takes
   a slot no module wanted and never displaces one. The strip is assembled in
   the catalogue, which is where that rule lives; this template lays out what
   it is handed and counts nothing.

   A SLOT NOBODY FILLED SAYS SO, and that is what makes the empty dashboard a
   report rather than a broken page: four cards reading "nothing measured" is
   an answer; four cards missing is a page somebody has to debug. It cannot
   name the module that WOULD fill it — the host knows no module — so it says
   what it can, which is that nothing here publishes one. #}
<div class="grid kstrip ao-kstrip" data-w="kpis">
    {% for tile in figures %}
        <div class="c kpi{{ tile.tone == 'hot' ? ' hot' : '' }}{{ tile.tone == 'bad' ? ' bad' : '' }}">
            <span class="tab">{{ tile.label }}{% if tile.live %}<span class="src">&middot; <span class="ao-live"><i></i></span></span>{% endif %}</span>
            <b class="disp">{{ tile.value }}{% if tile.unit %}<em>{{ tile.unit }}</em>{% endif %}</b>
            {% if tile.subline %}<span class="sub">{{ tile.subline }}{% if tile.alarm %} &middot; <span class="r">{{ tile.alarm }}</span>{% endif %}</span>{% endif %}
        </div>
    {% endfor %}
    {% if figures|length < 4 %}
        {% for _ in 1..(4 - figures|length) %}
            <div class="c kpi">
                <span class="tab">Nothing measured</span>
                <b class="disp">&mdash;</b>
                <span class="sub">nothing measured &middot; no module publishes this</span>
            </div>
        {% endfor %}
    {% endif %}
</div>
